[FINAL-TEXT] I now have complete, direct evidence. Here is the Phase 5 diagnosis.

=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router1
diagnosis_text:
**Affected component:** BGP neighbor configuration under `router bgp 1` on router1 — the `neighbor` statement for the eBGP peer is entirely absent.

**Mechanism:** BGP requires both endpoints to have each other explicitly declared as a neighbor. router2 (AS 2) is correctly configured with `neighbor 193.10.11.1 remote-as 1`, actively trying to open a TCP session on port 179 toward router1's eth0 address. Because router1 has no `neighbor` statement at all in its `router bgp 1` block, it neither initiates a connection to router2 nor accepts inbound BGP TCP connections from 193.10.11.2 (unknown peer). The session therefore stays perpetually in `Active` on router2's side. Without an established eBGP session, no routes are exchanged: router1 never installs a route to 200.1.1.0/24, and router2 never installs a route to 195.11.14.0/24. Packets from pc1 toward pc2 (200.1.1.2) hit router1, which has no matching route and returns ICMP "Destination Net Unreachable" — exactly the symptom observed. The reverse path fails identically.

**Localization:** router1, `router bgp 1` configuration block in FRR (`bgpd`). The inter-router link (router1:eth0 ↔ router2:eth0, 193.10.11.0/24) is physically UP and properly addressed; only the BGP peer declaration is missing.

**Root cause:** router1's FRR BGP configuration is missing the `neighbor 193.10.11.2 remote-as 2` statement, so it has zero configured BGP neighbors and the eBGP session to router2 can never form.

**Fix:** Add `neighbor 193.10.11.2 remote-as 2` under `router bgp 1` on router1 (via `vtysh`). Once the session establishes, both routers will exchange their `network` prefixes (195.11.14.0/24 and 200.1.1.0/24) and cross-AS reachability will be restored.
=== END ===